Code-generation approaches achieve type compliance via format constraints but produce 45\% empty outputs on zero-shot RE (vs.\ 0\% for OntoCoDec), reflecting a coverage--compliance trade-off that decoding-time enforcement avoids.

\paragraph{Constraint enforcement in IE.}
\citet{roth_yih_2004} first formulated hard type constraints for IE via ILP; post-hoc validation~\cite{khorshidi2025odke,spires_2024,ontologx_2025} detects violations after generation, requiring rejection cycles.
EnriCo~\cite{zaratiana2024enrico} and IterGen~\cite{ugare2025itergen} enforce constraints via ASP or backtracking; BoostCD~\cite{boostcd2025} combines constrained and unconstrained outputs via boosting on closed IE benchmarks (different benchmarks preclude direct comparison); DCCD~\cite{dccd2026} explores draft-conditioned hybrid approaches.
OntoCoDec differs in enforcement mechanism: \emph{preventive} forward-only token masking at $O(|V|)$ per step with no backtracking, vs.\ IterGen's \emph{corrective} backtracking (broader constraint classes but retry cost) and PICARD's~\cite{scholak2021picard} post-hoc parsing (monitor, not generative constraint).
This trades constraint expressiveness (Levels~1--2 only) for guaranteed single-pass efficiency.
Constrained decoding can degrade reasoning~\cite{banerjee2025crane,schall2025hiddencost,formattax2026}; however, extraction tasks are robust to format constraints~\cite{tam2024letmespeak}, and OntoCoDec constrains only type tokens.

% Section: Constraint Composition (compressed, 787→~500 words)

At each decoding step $t$, let $G(t) \subseteq V$ and $S(t) \subseteq V$ denote the tokens permitted by the grammar and semantic layers, respectively.
The final mask is their conjunction:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:composition}
  M_{\text{final}}(t) = M_{\text{grammar}}(t) \;\wedge\; M_{\text{semantic}}(t),
\end{equation}
%
where $\wedge$ denotes element-wise AND over the vocabulary $V$; a token is allowed only if it satisfies \emph{both} structural and type-level constraints.
The conjunction is asymmetric: $S(t)$ is defined only over $G(t)$. This reflects the enabling dependency (\S\ref{sec:method}): the semantic layer requires the relation type to belong to $\mathcal{R}$, a property guaranteed only by the grammar layer.

\paragraph{Grammar-First Fallback.}
When $S(t) \cap G(t) = \emptyset$ (tight constraints leave no token that is both grammar-valid and semantically valid), na\"{\i}ve masking would force selection of a grammar-invalid token, breaking JSON structure.
We enforce a \emph{grammar-first fallback}: if no semantically valid token survives grammar filtering, semantic masking is skipped and the grammar layer alone controls selection.
Structural integrity is a hard constraint; a type violation is a soft error detectable by downstream validation.
Among type violations, DR and CF differ in severity: a DR violation renders the triple ontologically invalid (the entity type lies outside the relation's domain or range), whereas a CF violation means each triple is individually schema-valid but the same entity receives different type assignments across triples.

\paragraph{Constraint Tightness.}
Recall the tightness of relation $r$ (\S\ref{sec:bg:ontology}):\footnote{For symmetric relations, $|\sigma(r)|$ counts ordered pairs; the normalization $|\mathcal{E}|^2$ remains appropriate since we evaluate on directed triples.}
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:tightness}
  \kappa(r) = 1 - \frac{|\sigma(r)|}{|\mathcal{E}|^2},
\end{equation}
%
The mean $\bar{\kappa} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{R}|}\sum_{r} \kappa(r)$ characterizes schema restrictiveness; tighter constraints force more frequent fallback and higher residual DR.
Cross-dataset results align: SciERC ($\bar{\kappa}{=}0.333$) achieves 94--99\% DR reduction vs.\ DocRED ($\bar{\kappa}{=}0.842$) at 45--90\% (full range 42--99\% across three models; Appendix~\ref{app:dual-factor}).
$\kappa$ is necessary but not sufficient---entity type confusability is the critical co-factor (Appendix~\ref{app:kappa-case-study})---and $\bar{\kappa}$ provides deployment guidance ($\bar{\kappa}{<}0.5$: near-complete DR elimination; $\bar{\kappa}{>}0.8$: requires fine-tuning; Appendix~\ref{app:tcr}).

\paragraph{Greedy limitation and fallback cascade.}
\label{sec:greedy-limitation}
The semantic layer commits entity types greedily---an instance of Level~3 (non-local) constraints (\S\ref{sec:decidability})---so CF violations arise when the same entity receives different types across triples.
Beam search (\S\ref{sec:beam-results}) resolves this by exploring multiple paths; TCR (Appendix~\ref{app:tcr}) offers a post-hoc repair.
Fallback frequency correlates with $\bar{\kappa}$: SciERC ($\bar{\kappa}{=}0.333$) triggers fallback rarely (94\% DR reduction); DocRED ($\bar{\kappa}{=}0.842$) more often (45\%); fine-tuning narrows the fallback regime to DR\,$\leq$\,0.09\% on both.
% [PLACEHOLDER: Per-relation fallback frequency data from Agent]

\paragraph{Non-rectangularity.}
\label{sec:rectangularity}
Sequential masking is necessary because type signatures are non-rectangular (0/7 SciERC, 23/96 DocRED relations satisfy $\sigma(r) = \operatorname{dom}(r) \times \operatorname{range}(r)$); independent enforcement would admit 33--37\% spurious pairs.

